¶
⟦300⟧{{smaller block|The surface value of the potential of the forces which would maintain the ocean in equilibrium in the shape it has at any moment is <math>\Sigma gb_i\mathfrak{b}_i</math>. Hence it follows that in the actual case the forces due to fluid pressure and to the attraction of the ocean must be such as to balance the potential just determined. Therefore these forces are those due to a potential—<math>\Sigma gb_i\mathfrak{b}_i</math>. If we add to this the potential of the external forces, we have a potential which will include all the forces, the expression for which is <math>-g\Sigma b_i(\mathfrak{b}_i-\mathfrak{e}_i)</math>. If further we perform the operations <math>d/ad\theta</math> and <math>d/a\sin\theta d\phi</math> on this potential, we obtain the colatitudinal and longitudinal forces which are equal to the accelerations <math>\Xi</math> and <math>\mathrm{H}</math>.}}

⟦600⟧It follows, then, from (14) that the equations of motion are
{{block center|<math>\frac{d^2\xi}{dt^2}-2n\sin\theta\cos\theta\frac{d\eta}{dt}=-\frac{g}{a}\Sigma b_i\frac{d}{d\theta}\left(\mathfrak{b}_i-\mathfrak{e}_i\right)</math>}}
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Equations of Motion.}}
{{block center|<math>\sin\theta\frac{d^2\eta}{dt^2}+2n\cos\theta\frac{d\xi}{dt}=-\frac{g}{a\sin\theta}\Sigma b_i\frac{d}{d\phi}\left(\mathfrak{b}_i-\mathfrak{e}\right)</math> (15)}}

⟦800⟧{{smaller block|It remains to find the equation of continuity. This may be deduced geometrically from the consideration that the volume of an element of the fluid remains constant; but a shorter way is to derive it from the equation of continuity as it occurs in ordinary hydrodynamical investigations. If <math>\Phi</math> be a velocity potential, the equation of continuity for incompressible fluid is
{{block center|<math>\delta r\frac{d}{dr}\left(r^2\frac{d\Phi}{dr}\sin\theta\,\delta\theta\,\delta\phi\right)+\delta\theta\frac{d}{d\theta}\left(r\sin\theta\frac{d\Phi}{rd\theta}\delta r\delta\phi\right)</math>}}
{{block center|<math>+\delta\phi\frac{d}{d\phi}\left(r\frac{1}{r\sin\theta}\frac{d\Phi}{d\phi}\delta r\,\delta\theta\right)=0.</math>}}}}

⟦1100⟧{{smaller block|The element referred to in this equation is defined by <math>r</math>, <math>\theta</math>, <math>\phi</math>, <math>r+\delta r</math>, <math>\theta+\delta\theta</math>, <math>\phi+\delta\phi</math>. The colatitudinal and longitudinal velocities are the same for all the elementary prism defined by <math>\theta</math>, <math>\phi</math>, <math>\theta+\delta\theta</math>, <math>\phi+\delta\phi</math>, and the sea bottom. Then <math>\frac{d\Phi}{rd\theta}=\frac{d\xi}{dt}</math>, <math>\frac{d\Phi}{r\sin\theta d\phi}=\sin\theta\frac{d\eta}{dt}</math>; and, since the radial velocity is <math>d\mathfrak{b}/dt</math> at the surface of the ocean, where <math>r=a+\gamma</math>, and is zero at the sea bottom, where <math>r=a</math>, we have <math>\frac{d\Phi}{dr}=\frac{r-a}{\gamma}\frac{d\mathfrak{b}}{dt}</math>. Hence, integrating with respect to <math>r</math> from <math>r=a+\gamma</math> to <math>r=a</math>, and again with respect to <math>t</math> from time <math>t</math> to the time when <math>\mathfrak{b}</math>, <math>\xi</math>, <math>\eta</math> all vanish, and treating <math>\gamma</math> and <math>\mathfrak{h}</math> as small compared with <math>a</math>, we have
{{block center|<math>\mathfrak{b}a\sin\theta+\frac{d}{d\theta}(\gamma\xi\sin\theta)+\frac{d}{d\phi}(\gamma\eta\sin\theta)=0.</math> (16)}}}}

⟦1700⟧{{smaller block|This is the equation of continuity, and, together with (15), it forms the system which must be integrated in the
{{EB1911 Shoulder Heading|Equation of Continuity.}}
general problem of the tides. The difficulties in the way of a solution are so great that none has hitherto been found, except on the supposition that <math>\gamma</math>, the depth of the ocean, is only a function of latitude. In this case (16) becomes
{{block center|<math>\mathfrak{b}a+\frac{1}{\sin\theta}\frac{d}{d\theta}(\gamma\xi\sin\theta)+\gamma\frac{d\eta}{d\phi}=0.</math> (17)}}}}

⟦2000⟧{{smaller block|§ 16. ''Adaptation to Forced Oscillations.''—Since we may suppose that the free oscillations are annulled by friction, the solution required is that corresponding to forced oscillations. Now we have seen from (13) that <math>\mathfrak{e}</math> (which is proportional to <math>V</math>) has terms of three kinds, the first depending on twice the moon's (or sun's) hour-angle, the second on the hour-angle, and the third independent thereof. The coefficients of the first and second vary slowly, and the whole of the third varies slowly. Hence <math>\mathfrak{e}</math> has a semi-diurnal, a diurnal and a long-period term. We shall see later that these terms may be expanded in a series of approximately semi-diurnal, diurnal and slowly varying terms, each of which is a strictly harmonic function of the time.}}

⟦2400⟧{{smaller block|Thus according to the usual method of treating oscillating systems, we may make the following assumptions as to the form of the solution
{{block center|<math>\mathfrak{e}=\Sigma\mathfrak{e}_i=\Sigma e_i\cos(2nft+s\phi+\alpha)</math>}}
{{block center|<math>\mathfrak{b}=\mathfrak{b}\Sigma_i=\Sigma h_i\cos(2nft+s\phi+\alpha)</math> (18)}}
{{block center|<math>\xi=\Sigma b_ix_i\cos(2nft+s\phi+\alpha)</math>}}
{{block center|<math>\eta=\Sigma b_iy_i\sin(2nft+s\phi+\alpha)</math>}}
where <math>e_i</math>, <math>h_i</math>, <math>x_i</math>, <math>y_i</math> are functions of colatitude only, and <math>e_i</math>, <math>h_i</math> are the associated functions of colatitude corresponding to the harmonic of order <math>i</math> and rank <math>s</math>.}}

⟦2700⟧For the semi-diurnal tides <math>s=2</math> and <math>f</math> is approximately unity; for the diurnal tides <math>s=1</math> and <math>f</math> is approximately <math>\tfrac{1}{2}</math>; and for the tides of long period <math>s=0</math> and <math>f</math> is a small fraction.

⟦2800⟧Substituting these values in (17) we have
{{block center|<math>\Sigma\left[\frac{1}{\sin\theta}\frac{d}{d\theta}(\gamma b_ix_i\sin\theta)+s\gamma b_iy_i+h_ia\right]=0.</math> (19)}}

⟦2900⟧Then if we write <math>u_i</math> for <math>h_i-e_i</math>, and put <math>m=n^2a/g</math>, substitution from (18) in (15) leads at once to
{{block center|<math>f^2\Sigma b_ix_i-f\sin\theta\cos\theta\Sigma b_iy_i=\frac{1}{4m}\frac{d}{d\theta}\Sigma b_iu_i,</math>}}
{{block center|<math>f^2\sin\theta\Sigma b_iy_i+f\cos\theta\Sigma b_ix_i=-\frac{s}{4m\sin\theta}\Sigma b_iu_i.</math> (20)}}
